{White's opening leaves a great deal to be desired. 10 f4 was extremely
premature. The second game in a row in which White has left his kingside
under-developed. In fact it has been a feature of his tournament so far that
Black has been coming out of the opening exceptionally well. This has been
commented on regarding British chess before. Black's atttractive pawn
sacrifice did not appear to lead anywhere. 17...Qe7 looks better, answering 18
d6 with 18...Bxc3. At least Black can equalize fully then. White did well not
to try to hand on to his extra pawn with 26 dxc6 as then the bishops become
extremely strong. Black seemed to haed blithely or a bishops of opposite
colour ending which was extremely difficult for him. It is not clear whether
he could have held it but there was some time wasted in the move in the late
30s. One revealing comment by Mark Hebden when Nigel Davies was examining one
onvoluted line: "Oh, I only looked at the variation we actually played". But
46...f5 was the last straw, fixing the pawn on a white square. A much-flawed
but interesting game.} 1. e4 g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3. d4 d6 4. Nge2 a6 5. a4 Nc6 6. Be3
e5 7. h3 Nge7 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Nd4 O-O 10. f4 N5c6 11. Bc4 d5 12. exd5 Nxd4 13.
Bxd4 Nf5 14. Bf2 Re8+ 15. Kf1 Ne3+ 16. Bxe3 Rxe3 17. Qd2 Re8 18. Re1 Rxe1+ 19.
Kxe1 b5 20. Bb3 b4 21. Nd1 Qh4+ 22. Qf2 Qe7+ 23. Kd2 Bb7 24. Kc1 Qd6 25. Qf3 c6
26. Nf2 cxd5 27. Re1 a5 28. Nd3 Rd8 29. Qe2 Ba6 30. Qe7 Qxe7 31. Rxe7 Bxd3 32.
cxd3 Bd4 33. Kb1 Rd6 34. Re8+ Kg7 35. Ra8 Re6 36. Bxd5 Re2 37. b3 Bc3 38. d4
Rd2 39. Rxa5 Bxd4 40. Rb5 Bc3 41. a5 Rb2+ 42. Kc1 Ra2 43. Bb7 Kf6 44. a6 Ke7
45. Rd5 Ke6 46. g4 f5 47. gxf5+ gxf5 48. Rb5 1-0

