{White's opening choice never really impresses. Black obtained a significant
plus but ran short of time. Davies, probably somewhat relieved at getting away
with it, accepted Watson's draw offer with alacrity. However, it is by no
means clear who stands better in the final position. I think that I eventually
convinced the players in the post-mortem that 28...Qxb5, or perhaps 27...Rc7,
preventing the White queen getting among the pigeons at d7, was more discreet.
} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Bd7 4. a4 Nf6 5. d3 Nc6 6. O-O g6 7. Nbd2 Bg7 8.
