{Mark's ames are seldom dull and this was no exception. Does he play f4 in all
his games? Black's play was particularly enterprising and the opening is most
intriguing. That's an uninformative comment for you! 12 Qc3 is another
tactical possibility. Clearly Black stood considerably better but White still
has a wide range of choices at move 26. Probably Bh3 is best but Nd6 is also
possible. However, Rc1 would leave White with an unpleasant heterogeneous
position.Black lost his way with the cautious 28...b6, 28...Ng4 is almost
impossible to answer. Once again, mark wriggles out of his difficulties by
active defence.} 1. e4 g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3. f4 c6 4. d4 d6 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. Be3 Qb6 7.
