{William's game plan was to keep the position simple and the draw in hand. A
case of the operation bein a failure but the patient surviving. 6...c5 is more
normal. 13 e5 was extremely plausible. 15...exf4 16 Bxf4 Ne6 17 Bxd6 Neg5 18
Bxf8 Bxf8 would give considerable compensation for the exchange. 24 Re1 was
much better than c5, White overlooked the force of Qe3.} 1. c4 g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3.
g3 d6 4. Bg2 Nf6 5. e4 O-O 6. Nge2 Be6 7. d3 Qc8 8. h3 c6 9. Be3 Na6 10. Rc1
Nc7 11. g4 Bd7 12. f4 h5 13. g5 Nh7 14. Nd4 e5 15. Nf3 Re8 (15... exf4 16. Bxf4
Ne6 17. Bxd6 Nexg5 18. Bxf8 Bxf8 $44) 16. f5 gxf5 17. exf5 Bxf5 18. Nh4 Bg6 19.
O-O Ne6 20. Nxg6 fxg6 21. h4 Nd4 22. Ne4 Qe6 23. Bxd4 exd4 24. c5 d5 25. Nf2
Qe3 26. Qb3 Rab8 27. Rce1 Qf4 28. Ne4 Qxh4 29. Nd6 Rf8 30. Bxd5+ cxd5 31. Qxd5+
Kh8 32. Nf7+ Rxf7 33. Qxf7 Qxg5+ 34. Kh1 Qh4+ 35. Kg2 Qg5+ 36. Kh1 Be5 37. Re4
Rf8 38. Qxf8+ Nxf8 39. Rxf8+ Kg7 40. Re1 Bf6 {Congratulations to William on
winning the tournament with a round to spare, and also on learning how to
write a legible scoresheet. It is noticeable that, since learning to do the
latter, his standard of play has risen remarkably.} 0-1

